# The :steal lane policy, which no other suite exercises.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The :steal path offloads a spawn to the shared overflow
# queue with bender_channel_push(..., 2) and reads the result as the try-push
# convention "0 = refused, nonzero = enqueued" (src/core/proc.c). Mode 2 is
# NOT the try-push: it is choice-write (BENDER_CP_MODE_CHOICE_WRITE, select
# semantics) whose pushes ignore their return value, while MODE 3 is the
# try-push that reports a full queue without buffering and unpacks the marshal
# buffer on refusal. Using mode 2 there is the same misuse that crashed a
# spawn-batching prototype: the receiving lane's unmarshalled copy pointed into
# a dead marshal buffer, and the next collection to walk it died in
# bender_mark_fiber on a garbage abstract type pointer.
#
# The overflow path is entered whenever the target ctl has ANY queued message
# (`bender_channel_count(ctl) >= 1`), so sustained spawning under :steal routes
# nearly every spawn through it. :steal is not the default policy, which is why
# this was never covered.
#
# MEASURED RESULT: the mode-2 push is NOT currently reachable, so this is a
# regression guard rather than a bug reproducer. The overflow path is gated on
# `bender_channel_count(ctl) >= 1`, and the lane dispatcher sits parked on
# (ev/take ctl), so a push hands off directly to the parked reader and the
# queue count never rises above zero. Measured :overflow-pushed 0 at 20000
# procs / 4 lanes, 5000 / 4 lanes, and 3000 / 1 lane with deliberately busy
# proc bodies. So :steal never actually steals: it behaves as round-robin.
#
# This suite therefore pins TWO things. It covers a policy that had no
# coverage at all, and it prints the overflow counters so that if anyone makes
# the path live, the mode-2 misuse above becomes visible here instead of
# surfacing as a premature free in a collector walk.
#
# N is held at 20000 on purpose: at 100000 with 4 lanes this aborts with
# "failed to write event to self-pipe", which is the documented self-pipe
# ceiling in docs/internals/proc-spawn-backpressure.md ("50000 and 80000
# complete, 100000 aborts"), not a fault of this policy.

(import ./helper :prefix "" :exit true)
(start-suite)

(def N 20000)

(proc/lanes-start 4)
(assert (= :steal (proc/lanes-policy :steal)) "steal policy selected")

(def done (ev/thread-chan 1024))

(var i 0)
(while (< i N)
  (proc/spawn (fn [] (ev/give done :ok)) 8)
  (set i (+ i 1)))

# Collect every result. A premature free of a spawn payload loses procs, so a
# short count is a real failure rather than a timing artifact.
(var got 0)
(while (< got N)
  (ev/take done)
  (set got (+ got 1)))

(assert (= N got) "every :steal-routed proc ran and reported")

# Recorded, not asserted: a nonzero push count means the overflow path went
# live and the mode-2 vs mode-3 question above now needs answering.
(def stats (proc/lanes-stats))
(printf "steal-overflow stats: %j" stats)

(proc/lanes-stop)
(end-suite)
